<!-- @after 7. Deep Dive: Token Lifecycle (15 min) :: animation -->
### Follow two requests through one token refresh

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated><span class="stepper-title">Two 401s, one refresh</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="3"><div class="node" data-el="reqA" data-art="code" data-art-role="client"><strong>Request A</strong><small data-text="reqAV">GET /orders</small></div><div class="node" data-el="reqB" data-art="code" data-art-role="client"><strong>Request B</strong><small data-text="reqBV">GET /v1/me</small></div><div class="node" data-el="ref" data-art="lock" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>TokenRefresher</strong><small data-text="refV">Mutex + vault re-read</small></div></div><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="3"><div class="node" data-el="idp" data-art="server" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>IdP</strong><small data-text="idpV">POST /token</small></div><div class="node" data-el="vault" data-art="key" data-art-role="storage"><strong>TokenVault</strong><small data-text="vaultV">access token T1</small></div><div class="node" data-el="api" data-art="server" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Resource API</strong><small data-text="apiV">validates Bearer</small></div></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="event" data-art="signal" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>What happens</strong><small data-text="eventV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
  {
    "label": "Stale token sent",
    "caption": "The device clock runs behind, so the access token T1 still looks valid locally. SessionAuthInterceptor attaches T1 to two GET requests that leave at the same time on OkHttp threads.",
    "add": [
      "reqA",
      "reqB",
      "ref",
      "idp",
      "vault",
      "api",
      "event"
    ],
    "state": {
      "reqA": "active",
      "reqB": "active"
    },
    "text": {
      "eventV": "both send Bearer T1"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Both get 401",
    "caption": "The resource API is the truth about expiry and rejects T1 on both requests. Each interceptor closes its response and calls refreshNow(failedAccessToken = T1).",
    "state": {
      "api": "error",
      "reqA": "warn",
      "reqB": "warn"
    },
    "text": {
      "apiV": "401 · 401",
      "eventV": "two callers want a refresh"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Single flight",
    "caption": "A’s refresh takes the Mutex first and sends POST /token with the refresh token. B’s refresh waits on the same lock instead of sending a second request.",
    "state": {
      "ref": "active",
      "idp": "active"
    },
    "text": {
      "refV": "A holds the lock · B waits",
      "eventV": "one refresh in flight"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "ref",
      "to": "idp",
      "kind": "parcel",
      "label": "refresh token"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Caller cancelled",
    "caption": "Request A’s caller is cancelled while A owns the refresh. The bounded rotation and commit run under NonCancellable, so cancellation waits for them. There is no hidden app-scope job.",
    "state": {
      "reqA": "warn"
    },
    "text": {
      "reqAV": "cancelled, commit first",
      "eventV": "the started rotation finishes"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Rotation saved",
    "caption": "The IdP rotates the refresh token and returns T2. The refresher saves both new tokens to the vault before it releases the lock, then A’s cancellation takes effect. A lost response or process death can still leave recovery to the IdP’s policy.",
    "state": {
      "idp": "done",
      "vault": "done",
      "reqA": "ghost"
    },
    "text": {
      "reqAV": "cancelled after commit",
      "vaultV": "T2 + new refresh token",
      "eventV": "saved before unlock"
    },
    "handoff": {
      "from": "idp",
      "to": "vault",
      "kind": "rows",
      "label": "new tokens"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "No second refresh",
    "caption": "B gets the lock and reloads the vault. The access token is no longer T1, so another caller already refreshed, and B returns T2 without calling the IdP.",
    "state": {
      "ref": "done"
    },
    "text": {
      "refV": "lock released",
      "eventV": "one POST /token in total"
    }
  },
  {
    "label": "Retry once",
    "caption": "B’s interceptor retries its bodyless read once with T2 and gets 200. A write’s 401 returns to its caller. A confirmed invalid_grant ends only this login session; a transient refresh failure keeps its tokens.",
    "state": {
      "reqB": "done",
      "api": "done",
      "event": "done"
    },
    "text": {
      "reqBV": "200 with T2",
      "apiV": "accepts T2",
      "eventV": "retried once"
    }
  }
]</script><figcaption>The Mutex makes the refresh single-flight, the vault check stops a second POST /token, and NonCancellable finishes a started rotation when its caller is cancelled.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 12. Further Reading :: recap -->
### Say these in the interview

- **Sign in with Authorization Code and PKCE** in the system browser or a Custom Tab; the app never sees the password, and the redirect is a verified HTTPS App Link.
- **Make refresh single-flight.** A Mutex serializes refreshes, and a caller refreshes only if the vault still holds the token that failed.
- **Let the caller own the refresh.** Finish a started rotation and its commit under `NonCancellable`, so caller cancellation cannot lose it. This does not protect against process death.
- **Treat `exp` as advisory.** The server is the truth, and a 401 on a bodyless read triggers one refresh and one retry. A write’s 401 returns to its caller.
- **Only a confirmed `invalid_grant` ends the session.** End that login locally, clear only its data and cancel its work. A transient failure keeps its tokens.
- **Gate sensitive actions with biometric re-authentication** that stays valid for a short window, such as 5 minutes.
